  "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $P$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\overset{\\to }{PT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/6bfe5ad7a1695d5208504f1666014737b020976d73419f4be273b5a84097e242.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PT}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TR}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073669/assets/86a8fb7563c4a8874fc6d9b460c8e93bcf2c96e1b4e63a4f56f49f7326d2dcca.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ} \\\\  & =b-c+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
